ts and  often you see that just a red you never  see a white coming out so this is  what's given people the idea that maybe  it's red wine principally that ancient  people would have drunk i happen to  disagree with that i think  it was very select  group maybe that had white wines because  you it's a  very small percentage of the grapes uh  have that albino mutation  and we do have evidence they'll be  coming to that suggests that white wine  is drunk as well  so then the juice was put into these  jars  and these are the famous canaanite jars  or amperas  and uh the fermentation is actually  carried out in those vessels and then  you had clay stoppers that were put on  and stamped with the name of the king  often later periods you have information  about  where the winery is located even the  name of the vintner  so you've got like the first wine labels  here that you're looking at it  and you can in some cases you can get a  very exact date  now they don't put a lot of information  about  the quality of the wine you know if you  want tasting notes  about all it goes as far as saying good  very good very very good and uh  that's about the extent of it but uh  you know how did i get involved in uh  studying ancient wine  and you know it was kind of a circuitous  route like you've already suggested you  know with  taking you know chemistry and uh you  know i minored in  english here at cornell and i am a  townie  uh so uh you know i have great uh  affection for this town actually  and um and i went to high school here  and it was during high school that  i sort of as i look back on it now is  where i sort of got my first  impetus maybe to pick up wine studies  because when i was 16 years old we went  off to germany  and for about two months traveling  uh bicycling around and uh we soon  discovered that beer was  less expensive than coca-cola so we  started drinking lots of beer  and the first night actually that we  were in germany  this is a long time ago we had din